<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<appendix xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="geometry-appendix-b">
  <title>Geometry for Post-primary School Mathematics</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>This reference is being rebuilt as native mathematical markup, with individual definitions, axioms, theorems, proofs and diagrams. The source numbering follows <url href="https://curriculumonline.ie/getmedia/ed7de7ab-2376-44f0-9c7c-b511e470c0a7/Appendix_B_English.pdf" visual="Geometry for Post-primary School Mathematics (NCCA, Appendix B)"/>. The official document remains the authority for exact wording and syllabus requirements.</p>
    <p><em>Reconstruction status:</em> the opening theory, length, angles and degree measurement are available below, including the first theorem and its proof. The remaining material is not yet transcribed. Full-page facsimiles are deliberately excluded from this semantic edition.</p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="geometry-app-b-terms">
    <title>Starting concepts and terminology</title>
    <p>Some concepts are taken as starting points rather than formally defined in terms of simpler geometric concepts. Appendix B identifies nine undefined terms: angle, degree, length, line, plane, point, ray, real number and set. An <term>axiom</term><idx>axiom</idx> is a starting assumption, whereas a <term>theorem</term><idx>theorem</idx> is established by deductive reasoning.</p>
    <p>A definition fixes the meaning of a term. A proof uses accepted assumptions and previously established results. A drawing can help us understand the argument, but measuring a drawing is not a substitute for proof.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="geometry-app-b-theory">
    <title>The Theory</title>
    <p>Line is short for straight line. Take a fixed plane, once and for all, and consider just lines that lie in it. The plane and the lines are sets of points. Each line is a subset of the plane, i.e. each element of a line is a point of the plane. Each line is endless, extending forever in both directions. Each line has infinitely-many points. The points on a line can be taken to be ordered along the line in a natural way. As a consequence, given any three distinct points on a line, exactly one of them lies between the other two. Points that are not on a given line can be said to be on one or other side of the line. The sides of a line are sometimes referred to as half-planes.</p>
    <p>Notation 1. We denote points by roman capital letters A, B, C, etc., and lines by lower-case roman letters l, m, n, etc.</p>
    <p>Axioms are statements we will accept as true.</p>
    <axiom xml:id="geometry-app-b-axiom-1"><title>Axiom 1 (Two Points Axiom)</title><statement><p>There is exactly one line through any two given points. (We denote the line through A and B by AB.)</p></statement></axiom>
    <definition xml:id="geometry-app-b-definition-1"><title>Definition 1</title><statement><p>The line segment [AB] is the part of the line AB between A and B (including the endpoints). The point A divides the line AB into two pieces, called rays. The point A lies between all points of one ray and all points of the other. We denote the ray that starts at A and passes through B by [AB. Rays are sometimes referred to as half-lines.</p></statement></definition>
    <p>Three points usually determine three different lines.</p>
    <definition xml:id="geometry-app-b-definition-2"><title>Definition 2</title><statement><p>If three or more points lie on a single line, we say they are collinear.</p></statement></definition>
    <definition xml:id="geometry-app-b-definition-3"><title>Definition 3</title><statement><p>Let A, B and C be points that are not collinear. The triangle ∆ABC is the piece of the plane enclosed by the three line segments [AB], [BC] and [CA]. The segments are called its sides, and the points are called its vertices (singular vertex).</p></statement></definition>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="geometry-app-b-length-distance"><title>Length and Distance</title>
    <p>We denote the set of all real numbers by <m>\mathbb{R}</m>.</p>
    <definition xml:id="geometry-app-b-definition-4"><title>Definition 4</title><statement><p>We denote the distance between the points A and B by <m>|AB|</m>. We define the length of the segment [AB] to be <m>|AB|</m>.</p></statement></definition>
    <p>We often denote the lengths of the three sides of a triangle by <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m>. The usual thing for a triangle ∆ABC is to take <m>a=|BC|</m>, i.e. the length of the side opposite the vertex A, and similarly <m>b=|CA|</m> and <m>c=|AB|</m>.</p>
    <axiom xml:id="geometry-app-b-axiom-2"><title>Axiom 2 (Ruler Axiom)</title><statement><p>The distance between points has the following properties:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><p>the distance <m>|AB|</m> is never negative;</p></li>
        <li><p><m>|AB|=|BA|</m>;</p></li>
        <li><p>if C lies on AB, between A and B, then <m>|AB|=|AC|+|CB|</m>;</p></li>
        <li><p>(marking off a distance) given any ray from A, and given any real number <m>k\geq 0</m>, there is a unique point B on the ray whose distance from A is <m>k</m>.</p></li>
      </ol></statement></axiom>
    <definition xml:id="geometry-app-b-definition-5"><title>Definition 5</title><statement><p>The midpoint of the segment [AB] is the point M of the segment with <m>|AM|=|MB|=\frac{|AB|}{2}</m>.</p></statement></definition>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="geometry-app-b-angles"><title>Angles</title>
    <definition xml:id="geometry-app-b-definition-6"><title>Definition 6</title><statement><p>A subset of the plane is convex if it contains the whole segment that connects any two of its points.</p></statement></definition>
    <p>For example, one side of any line is a convex set, and triangles are convex sets.</p>
    <p>We do not define the term angle formally. Instead we say: There are things called angles. To each angle is associated:</p>
    <ol><li><p>a unique point A, called its vertex;</p></li>
    <li><p>two rays [AB and [AC, both starting at the vertex, and called the arms of the angle;</p></li>
    <li><p>a piece of the plane called the inside of the angle.</p></li></ol>
    <p>An angle is either a null angle, an ordinary angle, a straight angle, a reflex angle or a full angle. Unless otherwise specified, you may take it that any angle we talk about is an ordinary angle.</p>
    <definition xml:id="geometry-app-b-definition-7"><title>Definition 7</title><statement><p>An angle is a null angle if its arms coincide with one another and its inside is the empty set.</p></statement></definition>
    <definition xml:id="geometry-app-b-definition-8"><title>Definition 8</title><statement><p>An angle is an ordinary angle if its arms are not on one line, and its inside is a convex set.</p></statement></definition>
    <definition xml:id="geometry-app-b-definition-9"><title>Definition 9</title><statement><p>An angle is a straight angle if its arms are the two halves of one line, and its inside is one of the sides of that line.</p></statement></definition>
    <definition xml:id="geometry-app-b-definition-10"><title>Definition 10</title><statement><p>An angle is a reflex angle if its arms are not on one line, and its inside is not a convex set.</p></statement></definition>
    <definition xml:id="geometry-app-b-definition-11"><title>Definition 11</title><statement><p>An angle is a full angle if its arms coincide with one another and its inside is the rest of the plane.</p></statement></definition>
      <definition xml:id="geometry-app-b-definition-12"><title>Definition 12 — Angle notation</title><statement><p>For non-collinear points <m>A,B,C</m>, the ordinary angle formed by rays from <m>A</m> through <m>B</m> and <m>C</m> is denoted by <m>\angle BAC</m> or <m>\angle CAB</m>. The middle letter is the vertex. The same notation is used for a straight angle when <m>A</m> lies between <m>B</m> and <m>C</m>. Greek letters may also name angles.</p></statement></definition>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="geometry-app-b-degrees"><title>Degrees</title>
    <p>Use <m>|\angle BAC|</m> for the numerical degree measure of an angle. Distinguish the angle itself from its measure.</p>
    <axiom xml:id="geometry-app-b-axiom-3"><title>Axiom 3 — Protractor Axiom</title><statement><p>Angle measures lie between <m>0^\circ</m> and <m>360^\circ</m>, with ordinary angles strictly between <m>0^\circ</m> and <m>180^\circ</m>. A straight angle measures <m>180^\circ</m>. Given a ray and an ordinary angle measure, a unique ray on each side of the associated line forms that angle. When <m>D</m> lies inside the ordinary angle <m>\angle BAC</m>,</p><me>|\angle BAC|=|\angle BAD|+|\angle DAC|.</me></statement></axiom>
    <p>A null angle measures <m>0^\circ</m>, a full angle <m>360^\circ</m>, and the reflex angle associated with an ordinary angle of measure <m>\theta</m> measures <m>360^\circ-\theta</m>.</p>
    <definition xml:id="geometry-app-b-definition-13"><title>Definition 13 — Angle bisector</title><statement><p>The ray from <m>A</m> through <m>D</m> bisects <m>\angle BAC</m> when it divides the angle into two equal parts:</p><me>|\angle BAD|=|\angle DAC|=\frac{|\angle BAC|}{2}.</me></statement></definition>
    <definition xml:id="geometry-app-b-definition-14"><title>Definition 14 — Right angle</title><statement><p>A right angle has measure <m>90^\circ</m>.</p></statement></definition>
    <definition xml:id="geometry-app-b-definition-15"><title>Definition 15 — Acute and obtuse angles</title><statement><p>An ordinary angle is acute when its measure is less than <m>90^\circ</m>, and obtuse when its measure is greater than <m>90^\circ</m>.</p></statement></definition>
    <definition xml:id="geometry-app-b-definition-16"><title>Definition 16 — Supplementary angles</title><statement><p>When <m>\angle BAC</m> is straight and <m>D</m> is not on line <m>BC</m>, the angles <m>\angle BAD</m> and <m>\angle DAC</m> are supplementary: their measures add to <m>180^\circ</m>.</p></statement></definition>
    <definition xml:id="geometry-app-b-definition-17"><title>Definition 17 — Perpendicular lines</title><statement><p>Two intersecting lines are perpendicular if they form a right angle at their point of intersection.</p></statement></definition>
    <definition xml:id="geometry-app-b-definition-18"><title>Definition 18 — Vertically opposite angles</title><statement><p>Suppose two lines <m>BC</m> and <m>DE</m> meet at <m>A</m>, with <m>A</m> between <m>B</m> and <m>C</m> and between <m>D</m> and <m>E</m>. The angles <m>\angle BAD</m> and <m>\angle CAE</m> are vertically opposite.</p></statement></definition>
    <figure xml:id="geometry-app-b-figure-1"><caption>Two intersecting lines: vertically opposite angles.</caption>
      <image width="65%"><description>Lines BC and DE intersect at A. B lies to the left, C to the right, D above right and E below left. The opposing angles BAD and CAE are marked with equal arcs.</description><latex-image-code><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (-3,0);
\coordinate (C) at (3,0);
\coordinate (D) at (2,1.5);
\coordinate (E) at (-2,-1.5);
\draw[thick] (B)--(C);
\draw[thick] (D)--(E);
\draw (0.65,0) arc[start angle=0,end angle=-143.13,radius=0.65];
\draw (-0.65,0) arc[start angle=180,end angle=36.87,radius=0.65];
\fill (A) circle (1.4pt);
\node[below right] at (A) {$A$};
\node[left] at (B) {$B$};
\node[right] at (C) {$C$};
\node[above right] at (D) {$D$};
\node[below left] at (E) {$E$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image-code></image>
    </figure>
    <theorem xml:id="geometry-app-b-theorem-1"><title>Theorem 1 — Vertically opposite angles</title><statement><p>Vertically opposite angles are equal in measure.</p></statement><proof><p>In <xref ref="geometry-app-b-figure-1"/>, use the straight angles on lines <m>BC</m> and <m>DE</m> and the angle-addition property of <xref ref="geometry-app-b-axiom-3" text="title"/>.</p><md><mrow>|\angle BAD|+|\angle BAE|&amp;=180^\circ,</mrow><mrow>|\angle CAE|+|\angle BAE|&amp;=180^\circ.</mrow></md><p>Subtracting the common angle measure gives</p><me>|\angle BAD|=|\angle CAE|.</me><p>The same reasoning applies to the other pair of vertically opposite angles.</p></proof></theorem>
    <definition xml:id="geometry-app-b-definition-19"><title>Definition 19 — Congruent triangles</title><statement><p>Two triangles are congruent when their corresponding side lengths and corresponding angle measures are equal. The order of the vertex names identifies the correspondence.</p></statement></definition>
  </section>
</appendix>
